.TH TILOG 1 "October 4, 2026" "tilog 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tilog \- a small, fast, read-only terminal viewer for live logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tilog
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fISOURCE\fR...]
.br
.I command
|
.B tilog
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tilog
is \fBtig\fR for logs: a terminal viewer for watching logs during a deployment and for
checking production errors afterwards. It feels like
.BR less (1)
and
.BR tail (1),
needs no configuration, and only reads: it never changes a log or a server, and installs nothing
on remote hosts.
.PP
Every source gets a tab. The overview (tab 0) shows all sources side by side and follows them
live. A filter opens a second tile next to a source, with the entries that match, and stepping
through the filter shows each match in its place in the source.
.PP
A source is a file, standard input, or any command that prints lines, so SSH, Docker, Kubernetes
and
.BR journalctl (1)
work like a local file. A command that ends or loses its connection is started again with
a growing delay, and only what was missed is asked for.
.SH SOURCES
.TP
.I path
A file. It is opened at its end, so a file of any size opens at once, and followed. Older lines
are read when scrolling up. Log rotation (a renamed or truncated file) is followed. A path
ending in \fB.gz\fR is unpacked with
.BR gzip (1).
.TP
.B \-
Standard input. This is the default when something is piped in and no other source is given.
.TP
\fBssh:\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR:\fI/path\fR
.BR tail (1)
\-F on a remote host, over
.BR ssh (1).
.TP
\fBssh:\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR:\fBdocker:\fIcontainer\fR
.B docker logs \-f
on a remote host.
.TP
\fBssh:\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR:\fBkube:\fR[\fInamespace\fR/]\fIpod\fR[/\fIcontainer\fR]
.B kubectl logs \-f
run on a remote host.
.TP
\fBdocker:\fIcontainer\fR
.B docker logs \-f
of a local container.
.TP
\fBkube:\fR[\fInamespace\fR/]\fIpod\fR[/\fIcontainer\fR]
.B kubectl logs \-f
(also \fBk8s:\fR).
.TP
\fBcmd:\fIcommand\fR
Any command that prints lines, for example \fBcmd:journalctl \-fu app\fR.
.TP
.I name
A name from the configuration file.
.PP
At most nine sources are open at once; the merged timeline is extra.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fINAME\fR
Load a saved session (see \fB:save\fR).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR \fICMD\fR
Follow the output of a command; the same as the source \fBcmd:\fICMD\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the built-in configuration (the colors) as a starting point for your own.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-man
Print this manual page. To install it for the current user:
.RS
.PP
.EX
tilog \-\-print\-man > ~/.local/share/man/man1/tilog.1
.EE
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a short help.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.SH KEYS
The keys are those of
.BR less (1)
where there is one. Press \fB?\fR in tilog for the list that belongs to the version in use.
.TP
\fBj k\fR, \fB\(ua \(da\fR
Scroll one line. Enter also scrolls down in a tab.
.TP
\fBSpace b\fR
Page down and up (also \fBf\fR, PageDown, PageUp).
.TP
\fBd u\fR
Half a page down and up.
.TP
\fBg G\fR
First line, and last line with following (also Home, End and \fBF\fR).
.TP
\fBh l\fR, \fB\(<- \(->\fR
Scroll sideways.
.TP
\fB/\fItext\fR
Search: mark the text in every window and jump to the first match. A pattern without capital
letters ignores case.
.TP
\fBn N\fR
Next and previous search match. In a filter pane: the next and previous entry of the filter (or
search hit, if a search is on), shown in the main pane. After scrolling the pane they start
from the top of what is shown.
.TP
\fB] [\fR
Next and previous error line (\fBERROR\fR, \fBlevel=error\fR, \fB[crit]\fR, HTTP 5xx, ...).
.TP
\fB&\fItext\fR
Filter: open a tile with the matching entries. An entry is a line with the lines that belong
to it, such as a stack trace.
.TP
.B :
Run a command. A menu lists them; Tab or Enter picks one, and Enter runs it.
.TP
\fBTab\fR, \fBShift+Tab\fR
Select the next and previous window.
.TP
.B Enter
On the overview, open the selected source in its tab.
.TP
\fB0\fR\-\fB9\fR, \fBAlt+\(<- \(->\fR
Switch tabs. 0 is the overview.
.TP
.B m
Jump to the merged timeline.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the search; then go back to the overview.
.TP
.B q
Back to the overview; on the overview, quit. Ctrl+C always quits.
.TP
mouse
A click selects a window or a tab, dragging selects text and copies it, the wheel scrolls.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are typed after \fB:\fR. A command that takes arguments waits for them when it is picked
from the menu.
.TP
.B :help
Show the help.
.TP
\fB:add\fR \fIpath\fR
Add a source: any of the forms under \fBSOURCES\fR.
.TP
.B :overview
Back to the overview of all sources.
.TP
\fB:filter\fR [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] \fItext\fR
New tile with the entries that match. \fB\-a\fR opens it on every source, \fB\-r\fR reads the text
as a regular expression, \fB\-i\fR ignores case.
.TP
\fB:highlight\fR [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fItext\fR]
Mark text in every window, like a search with flags. Without text it clears the marks.
.TP
\fB:group\fR [\fBauto\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fIregex\fR]
How lines form one entry: indented lines continue the one above (\fBauto\fR), none do (\fBoff\fR),
or only lines matching the regular expression start one.
.TP
\fB:merge\fR [\fIsource\fR...]
One tab with the sources interleaved by timestamp. Tab numbers or names; all by default.
.TP
\fB:offset\fR \fI+2h\fR|\fI\-30m\fR|\fI+90s\fR
Shift this source's timestamps, then merge again (clock skew, time zones).
.TP
\fB:write\fR [\fB\-f\fR] \fIfile\fR
Save the focused tile's lines to a file. A filter saves all its matches. \fB\-f\fR replaces an
existing file.
.TP
.B :close
Close the focused tile (a main tile closes its source).
.TP
\fB:save\fR [\fIname\fR], \fB:load\fR \fIname\fR, \fB:sessions\fR
Save, restore and list sessions: which sources are open, their filters, and the tab.
.TP
.B :reload
Reload the configuration without restarting.
.TP
\fB:follow\fR [\fBall\fR], \fB:pause\fR [\fBall\fR]
Follow the newest line, or stop where you are, in the focused tile or in every tile. A paused
view shows the lines it had; what arrives meanwhile appears when following resumes.
.TP
.B :top
Jump to the first line.
.TP
\fB:goto\fR \fIline\fR|\fItime\fR
Jump to a line number, or to a time such as \fB08:16\fR or \fB2026\-10\-03 08:16:50\fR. The line at
or after that time is shown.
.TP
.B :clear
In a source tab, close all filter tiles. On the overview, empty the selected view.
.TP
.B :quit
Exit.
.SH SSH LOGINS
A host that accepts your key or your agent just works. If a login is refused, \fBssh\fR may ask
its question (a password, a passphrase, a one-time code, an unknown host key) and the question
appears in the tile of that source, where it is answered; Enter sends the answer and Esc gives up.
The answer is kept in memory only, so that a dropped connection reconnects by itself. A wrong
answer is not tried again, so that the host is not hammered with failed logins. Questions need
OpenSSH 8.4 or later.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tilog/config.toml
Colors, and named sources. \fBtilog \-\-print\-config\fR prints the defaults. The directory is
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tilog
if that is set.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tilog/sessions/NAME.toml
Saved sessions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, no colors are used: what a color marked becomes reverse video or bold.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is.
.SH EXAMPLES
Two files and a container, side by side:
.PP
.EX
tilog app.log nginx.log docker:api
.EE
.PP
Docker and Kubernetes on other hosts, over ssh:
.PP
.EX
tilog ssh:web1:docker:api ssh:bastion:kube:shop/web\-0
.EE
.PP
Whatever another program prints:
.PP
.EX
kubectl logs \-f web\-0 | tilog
.EE
.PP
A compressed log from last week:
.PP
.EX
tilog app.log.2.gz
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR tig (1),
.BR lnav (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.SH BUGS
Report them at https://github.com/maxwellscode/tilog/issues.
